// java-rt.metajs -- JAVA'S OWN SEMANTICS, in MetaJS. Layer 2 of
// docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3, for the third language after the Lua pilot
// and PHP.
//
// languages/java-to-llvm-ir.abnf emits handle IR that calls 55 js_* externs over
// every tests/java-test-*.java; 29 of them are generic primitives
// languages/lib/runtime.c already provides (the C floor, layer 1). The other 26
// are here.
//
// Compile with:
//     mec languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf languages/lib/java-rt.metajs -q -rt-lib
// which is what tests/gen-java-rt-ll.sh does; the result is the checked-in
// languages/lib/java-rt.ll that java-to-llvm-ir.abnf links next to runtime.ll.
// -rt-lib exports every TOP-LEVEL `function js_*` below under its own C symbol,
// so nothing else in this file is visible to the linker - the helpers are
// deliberately named jv*/jl*/jp*.
//
// THE AUTHORITATIVE SPECIFICATION IS abnf/jsrtjava.go plus abnf/jsrtjvm.go and
// the js_jadd / js_jband / js_char / js_jchareq / js_jcharat / js_mcall /
// js_supercall / js_is_type / js_arr_new_n block of abnf/jsrt.go. This file
// matches them function for function; where it CANNOT, the site says so in full.
//
// ----- the value model -------------------------------------------------------
//
//     null / undefined     ==  a null reference
//     a boolean            ==  boolean
//     a PLAIN NUMBER       ==  an `int`, i.e. a SIGNED 32 BIT value. This is the
//                              Java invariant and it is deliberately NOT Go's.
//     a tag 13 box, w 8/16 ==  byte / short. si_norm never unboxes those widths,
//                              so the floor's sized integer IS the type.
//     a tag 13 box, w 64,
//        marked UNSIGNED   ==  long.  <-- SEE "THE LONG PROBLEM" BELOW.
//     a tag 14 box         ==  double / float, style 0 (Java). flo/floIs/floNum/
//                              floStr/floOp/floEq/floMax/floMin/floAbs.
//     a {__char} box       ==  char. The Go twin uses jsChar, a Go TYPE with its
//                              own js_typeof answer ("char"). Layer 2 cannot make
//                              a primitive type, so this is an OBJECT box - the
//                              same {__char: code} languages/java-interpreter.abnf
//                              already uses, so the shape is not invented here.
//                              It is SAFE because java-to-llvm-ir.abnf emits
//                              js_typeof for exactly one test (=== "function")
//                              and compares chars with js_jchareq / js_jvcmp,
//                              never with the floor's js_seq. Measured, not
//                              assumed: see the extern audit in
//                              docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3.
//     an {__isclass} object ==  a class descriptor, built by the emitter
//     any other object      ==  an instance / an array is a floor array
//
// ----- THE LONG PROBLEM, and how `sintRaw` removed it ------------------------
//
// Java's `long` must stay DISTINGUISHABLE from `int` at every value, because
// `1000000L * 1000000L` is 1000000000000 and `1000000 * 1000000` is -727379968.
// abnf/jsrtjava.go says so directly: jvBox() "is 'read this as a long': always a
// box, never a plain number".
//
// The floor's tag 13 will not do that on its own. si_norm - the ONE place the
// sized-integer invariant is applied - unboxes a SIGNED 64 bit value a double
// holds exactly, and every one of the eleven sint* host builtins goes through it.
// So `sint(0, 5, 64, 0)` is the plain number 5, indistinguishable from `int` 5.
//
// This file used to answer that by marking the box UNSIGNED - the one shape
// si_norm will not unbox - and then re-supplying the six operations whose SIGNED
// reading the floor would get wrong (`/ % >>`, compare, decimal text, double
// reading) as jlDiv / jlMod / jlSar / jlCmp / jlStr / jlNum. The floor now has
//
//     sintRaw(hi, lo, bits, unsigned)   si_trunc, then si_make: a tag 13 cell
//                                       ALWAYS, never si_norm's unboxing arm
//
// so a Java long is an HONEST {w: 64, u: 0} box, exactly as csharp-rt.metajs has
// carried one from the start, and all six go back to the floor: sintOp's `/`,
// `%` and `>>` branch on the cell's own signedness, sintCmp compares signed when
// the type is, sintStr prints the minus sign and sintNum reads the negative half.
//
// The swap is not free and the trade is named here rather than buried. What the
// unsigned box bought was `>>>`, which was sintOp code 10 DIRECT because a
// logical shift is what an unsigned cell already does; a signed cell shifts
// arithmetically, so `>>>` now costs jlShr - jlSar traded for jlShr, one helper
// for one helper. What it cost was every FLOOR extern that reads a tag 13 cell
// numerically: to_number of an unsigned 64 bit cell is its MAGNITUDE, so
// `(double) -1L` answered 1.8446744073709552E19 through js_jflo and the emitter
// owes a js_jvnum unwrap in front of it. With a signed cell that whole hazard
// class is gone from layer 2. (java-to-llvm-ir.abnf still EMITS js_jvnum,
// because llvm.Run runs abnf/jsrtjava.go, whose jvBox is unchanged.)
//
// THE ONE RULE THE SWAP IMPOSES, and it is the thing to get right: si_apply
// ends in si_norm, so a signed 64 bit RESULT that a double holds exactly comes
// back as a PLAIN NUMBER - an int, as far as everything below is concerned.
// Every long-producing sintOp therefore goes through jlOp, which re-boxes.
// csharp-rt.metajs spells the same rule csNorm.
//
// ----- MetaJS rules that shape the code below --------------------------------
//
// docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md: a local keeps the TYPE CLASS it is first assigned
// (a variable that holds a number in one branch and a box in another is declared
// `var x = anytype`); there is no ASI, so every `return` carries its value on the
// same line and a value-less early return is braced; no for..in, no splice, no
// split/join/lastIndexOf, indexOf ignores its from-index, array.length is not
// assignable, and there are no exponent literals.

// The shared layer-2 runtime (docs/runtime-merge-plan.md part A). It supplies
// the externs every language implements identically, parameterised by the rtk*
// hooks below. Same mechanism ruby uses for `import "./regex.js"`.
import "./runtime.metajs"

// The bodies java and csharp share and NOBODY ELSE DOES, in their own module so
// the other eight importers of runtime.metajs pay nothing for them. The measured
// slope of the live-body tax, and the difference list that made the merge two
// knobs, are both at the head of that file.
import "./runtime-jvm.metajs"

// ----- runtime-jvm.metajs's two knobs ----------------------------------------
function rtjkCmp(op, l, r) { return jvCmp(op, l, r) }
function rtjkFloOperand(v) { return jvmOperand(v) }

// ----- the rtk* hooks the shared runtime calls -------------------------------
function rtkStr(v) { return jpStr(v) }
function rtkNum(v) { return rtjNum(v) }
function rtkIsChar(v) { return jvIsChar(v) }
function rtkIsArr(v) { return Array.isArray(v) }
// js_supercall's one knob. Only the four languages whose emitter actually emits
// js_supercall define it; see the note there.
function rtkCall(f, self, argv) { return f.apply(undefined, argv) }
// js_pyset's two knobs.
function rtkIsDict(v) { return rtIsDictObj(v) }
function rtkDictFind(keys, k) { return rtDictFind(keys, k) }

// ===================================================== long, exactly ========

function jlZero() { return sintRaw(0, 0, 64, false) }
function jlOnes() { return sintRaw(4294967295, 4294967295, 64, false) }
function jlMaxL() { return sintRaw(2147483647, 4294967295, 64, false) }
function jlMinL() { return sintRaw(2147483648, 0, 64, false) }

// A long is the ONE sized integer of width 64; byte and short are 8 and 16.
function jvIsLong(v) {
    if (!sintIs(v)) { return false }
    return sintWidth(v) == 64
}

// jlOp is sintOp with the invariant put back. si_apply ends in si_norm, which
// UNBOXES a signed 64 bit result a double holds exactly - so `2L + 3L` would come
// back as the plain number 5, i.e. as an int. Every long-producing operation goes
// through here. sintHi / sintLo read the two 32 bit halves of a plain number's
// two's complement value just as they read a cell's, so the re-box is exact for
// the negative half too; csharp-rt.metajs's csNorm is the same three lines.
function jlOp(code, a, b) {
    var t = sintOp(code, a, b)
    if (sintIs(t)) { return t }
    return sintRaw(sintHi(t), sintLo(t), 64, false)
}

// Two's complement negation, which is 0 - x at 64 bits and therefore maps
// Long.MIN_VALUE to itself, exactly as Java's unary minus does.
function jlNeg(x) { return jlOp(1, jlZero(), x) }
function jlNot(x) { return jlOp(7, x, jlOnes()) }

// An integral double as a long box, saturating at the two ends the way
// jvNarrowFloat does. Between them sintHi / sintLo give the exact halves; above
// 2^53 the double is already inexact and this reproduces that inexactness rather
// than inventing digits.
function jlFromNum(v) {
    if (v >= 9223372036854775808) { return jlMaxL() }
    if (v < 0 - 9223372036854775808) { return jlMinL() }
    return sintRaw(sintHi(v), sintLo(v), 64, false)
}

// `>>>` is the LOGICAL shift, and the box is signed, so sintOp code 10 gives the
// arithmetic one. Reading the same bits through an UNSIGNED cell is what the
// floor's own `if (u)` arm of code 10 wants; the result is re-marked signed,
// because the type of a shift is the left operand's (JLS 15.19) and Java has no
// unsigned long. This is the ONE operation the unsigned representation got for
// free, and it is what jlSar traded away.
function jlShr(a, s) {
    var t = sintOp(10, sintRaw(sintHi(a), sintLo(a), 64, true), s)
    return sintRaw(sintHi(t), sintLo(t), 64, false)
}

// ===================================================== the char box =========
//
// rtCharBox / rtIsCharBox are lib/runtime.metajs's; jvChar and jvIsChar were
// byte-identical to csharp's csChar / csIsChar.

function jvChar(code) { return rtCharBox(code) }

function jvIsChar(v) { return rtIsCharBox(v) }

// ===================================================== the value readings ===


// rtjIsIntegral - an operand of INTEGER arithmetic; a char is one (Java's char is
// an integral type) and a double is not - is SHARED with csharp,
// lib/runtime-jvm.metajs. The two copies had ZERO differences.

// jvW: JLS 5.6.2 binary numeric promotion for two integral operands - long when
// either side is long, int otherwise. So byte + byte is an int.
function jvW(l, r) {
    if (jvIsLong(l)) { return 64 }
    if (jvIsLong(r)) { return 64 }
    return 32
}

// jvNarrowFloat, the w < 64 half: JLS 5.1.3 SATURATES at the INT range and only
// then truncates the bits, so (int) 1e20 is Integer.MAX_VALUE and (byte) 1e20 is
// (byte) Integer.MAX_VALUE == -1.
function jvSatInt(f) {
    if (f !== f) { return 0 }
    if (f >= 2147483648) { return 2147483647 }
    if (f < 0 - 2147483648) { return 0 - 2147483648 }
    return Math.trunc(f)
}

// jvNarrowFloat, the w == 64 half.
function jvSatLong(f) {
    if (f !== f) { return jlZero() }
    if (f >= 9223372036854775808) { return jlMaxL() }
    if (f < 0 - 9223372036854775808) { return jlMinL() }
    return jlFromNum(Math.trunc(f))
}

// jvToLong: the exact 64 bit reading of an INTEGRAL value, as a long box.
function jvToLongBox(v) {
    if (jvIsLong(v)) { return v }
    if (jvIsChar(v)) { return jlFromNum(v["__char"]) }
    if (floIs(v)) { return jvSatLong(floNum(v)) }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return jlFromNum(sintNum(v)) }
    if (typeof v == "number") { return jlFromNum(Math.trunc(v)) }
    if (v === true) { return jlFromNum(1) }
    return jlZero()
}

// The low 32 bits of any value, as an UNSIGNED number - the one reading every
// narrowing below is built on.
//
// A BOXED DOUBLE saturates (jvNarrowFloat, JLS 5.1.3) and a PLAIN NUMBER wraps.
// That asymmetry is the whole of jvNarrow's `if isFlo` in abnf/jsrtjava.go and
// it is easy to get wrong: reading a plain 2147483648 through the saturating
// path answers 2147483647 where the Go twin's int32(giVal(...)) answers
// -2147483648, and `2147483648 - 1` then differs by one. Measured, not guessed:
// that was the first defect the native build of tests/java-test-full.java found.
function jvLow32(v) {
    if (jvIsChar(v)) { return v["__char"] }
    if (floIs(v)) {
        var f = jvSatInt(floNum(v))
        return f < 0 ? f + 4294967296 : f
    }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintLo(v) }
    if (typeof v == "number") {
        var t = Math.trunc(v)
        if (t !== t) { return 0 }
        if (t == Infinity) { return 0 }
        if (t == 0 - Infinity) { return 0 }
        if (t >= 0 - 2147483648 && t <= 2147483647) { return t < 0 ? t + 4294967296 : t }
        return sintLo(jlFromNum(t))
    }
    if (v === true) { return 1 }
    return 0
}

// jvI: the int reading of an integral operand - the low 32 bits, signed.
function jvI(v) {
    var lo = jvLow32(v)
    if (lo >= 2147483648) { return lo - 4294967296 }
    return lo
}

// rtjNum, the double reading of anything numeric, is SHARED with csharp -
// lib/runtime-jvm.metajs. The two copies differed in the char test and the string
// coercion and BOTH differences were identity: jvIsChar and csIsChar are each
// `rtIsCharBox(v)`, and jpStrNum - deleted with this merge - was a one-line
// wrapper on parseFloat. Its sintIs arm reads a SIGNED 64 bit cell with sintNum
// (si_float / d_from_long); the long arm this used to need (jlNum) is the
// floor's own now.

// jvNarrow is (byte) / (short) / (int) / (long) or a declared integral type. An
// integral source truncates its bits; a floating point one saturates first.
function jvNarrow(v, w) {
    if (w == 64) {
        if (floIs(v)) { return jvSatLong(floNum(v)) }
        return jvToLongBox(v)
    }
    var lo = jvLow32(v)
    if (w == 32) {
        if (lo >= 2147483648) { return lo - 4294967296 }
        return lo
    }
    if (w == 16) { return sint(0, lo & 65535, 16, 0) }
    return sint(0, lo & 255, 8, 0)
}


// ===================================================== ArithmeticException ==

// The class descriptor js_jvexc hands over once at module init, so a divide by
// zero can THROW rather than abort. The arithmetic externs have no access to the
// program's scope, which is exactly why the emitter passes it in.
var jvArithExcCls = undefined

function jvThrowArith(msg) {
    if (jvArithExcCls === undefined) {
        fail("java: " + msg)
        return undefined
    }
    var o = {}
    o["__class"] = jvArithExcCls
    // __msg is the field the emitted builtin exception constructor writes and
    // getMessage() reads (emitBuiltinExc in java-to-llvm-ir.abnf).
    o["__msg"] = msg
    throw o
}

// ===================================================== the operators ========

// jvArith is one binary arithmetic or bitwise operator on two INTEGRAL operands.
// The int case is plain 32 bit arithmetic; only a long operand pays for the box.
function jvArith(op, l, r) {
    if (jvW(l, r) == 64) {
        var a = jvToLongBox(l)
        var b = jvToLongBox(r)
        if (op == "/") {
            if (sintCmp(b, jlZero()) == 0) { jvThrowArith("/ by zero") }
            return jlOp(3, a, b)
        }
        if (op == "%") {
            if (sintCmp(b, jlZero()) == 0) { jvThrowArith("/ by zero") }
            return jlOp(4, a, b)
        }
        if (op == "+") { return jlOp(0, a, b) }
        if (op == "-") { return jlOp(1, a, b) }
        if (op == "*") { return jlOp(2, a, b) }
        if (op == "&") { return jlOp(5, a, b) }
        if (op == "|") { return jlOp(6, a, b) }
        if (op == "^") { return jlOp(7, a, b) }
        fail("js_jvarith: unknown operator '" + op + "'")
        return undefined
    }
    var x = jvI(l)
    var y = jvI(r)
    if (op == "+") { return rtWrap32(x + y) }
    if (op == "-") { return rtWrap32(x - y) }
    if (op == "*") { return jvMul32(x, y) }
    if (op == "/") {
        if (y == 0) { jvThrowArith("/ by zero") }
        // Integer.MIN_VALUE / -1 overflows back to itself (JLS 15.17.2).
        if (x == 0 - 2147483648) { if (y == 0 - 1) { return x } }
        return rtWrap32(Math.trunc(x / y))
    }
    if (op == "%") {
        if (y == 0) { jvThrowArith("/ by zero") }
        if (x == 0 - 2147483648) { if (y == 0 - 1) { return 0 } }
        return x - Math.trunc(x / y) * y
    }
    if (op == "&") { return x & y }
    if (op == "|") { return x | y }
    if (op == "^") { return x ^ y }
    fail("js_jvarith: unknown operator '" + op + "'")
    return undefined
}

// int * int wraps at 32 bits, and the product of two int32 values is up to 62
// bits - beyond what a double multiplies exactly. Math.imul is the floor's own
// 32 bit multiply and is exact by construction.
function jvMul32(x, y) { return Math.imul(x, y) }

// jvShift is << >> >>>. The result type is the LEFT operand's alone (JLS 15.19),
// and the COUNT is MASKED - & 31 for an int, & 63 for a long. That is why
// `1 << 32` is 1 rather than 0, and it is the one place Java and Go disagree
// outright, Go shifting everything out instead.
function jvShift(op, l, r) {
    var n = jvI(r)
    if (jvIsLong(l)) {
        var s = n & 63
        var a = jvToLongBox(l)
        if (op == "<<") { return jlOp(9, a, s) }
        if (op == ">>") { return jlOp(10, a, s) }
        return jlShr(a, s)
    }
    var x = jvI(l)
    var t = n & 31
    if (op == "<<") { return x << t }
    if (op == ">>") { return x >> t }
    // `>>>` in MetaJS is JavaScript's, whose result is an UNSIGNED 32 bit value:
    // -1 >>> 0 is 4294967295 there and -1 in Java, where the result type is still
    // int. jvShift's Go twin says the same thing as int32(uint32(a) >> s).
    return rtWrap32(x >>> t)
}

// jvCmp is the ordered comparison at the PROMOTED width. A byte compares against
// an int at INT width - 5 < 1000 is not 5 < -24.
function jvCmp(op, l, r) {
    var c = 0
    if (jvW(l, r) == 64) {
        c = sintCmp(jvToLongBox(l), jvToLongBox(r))
    } else {
        var a = jvI(l)
        var b = jvI(r)
        if (a < b) { c = 0 - 1 } else if (a > b) { c = 1 }
    }
    if (op == "<") { return c < 0 }
    if (op == ">") { return c > 0 }
    if (op == "<=") { return c <= 0 }
    if (op == ">=") { return c >= 0 }
    if (op == "==") { return c == 0 }
    return c != 0
}

// rtjArith (abnf/jsrtjvm.go) - a double on either side makes the whole operation
// floating point and its result a double, two integral operands keep the 32 bit
// wrap - is SHARED with csharp, lib/runtime-jvm.metajs. Its ONE knob is the
// operand mapper below, reached through rtjkFloOperand.

// floOp reads its operands with the floor's to_number, which knows tag 13 and
// tag 14 but not the {__char} box - and reads an UNSIGNED 64 bit box as a
// magnitude, which a Java long is not. Both are converted here, so what reaches
// floOp is either a plain double or a tag 14 box.
function jvmOperand(v) {
    if (floIs(v)) { return v }
    return rtjNum(v)
}

// jvmTakeL is the OPERAND CHOICE of Math.max / Math.min, and the two cases a
// plain `>` / `<` cannot see. java.lang.Math documents both for the double
// overloads and java 24.0.2 confirms each: Math.min(-0.0, 0.0) is -0.0 and
// Math.max(-0.0, 0.0) is 0.0 ("if one argument is positive zero and the other
// negative zero, the result of min is negative zero", and of max positive
// zero), and Math.min(NaN, 1.0) is NaN ("if either value is NaN, then the
// result is NaN"). Both comparisons are FALSE, so a bare `<` took the right
// operand and answered 0.0 and 1.0. Everything else is the ordinary
// comparison, a tie still going to the right operand. The twins are
// abnf/jsrtjvm.go's jvmTakeL and languages/lib/runtime.c's jf_take_l.
function jvmTakeL(a, b, wantMax) {
    if (a !== a) { return true }
    if (b !== b) { return false }
    if (a == 0 && b == 0) {
        // Both zero and only the sign bit tells them apart: min wants the
        // negative one, max the positive one. 1/-0.0 is -Infinity.
        if (wantMax) { return 1 / b < 0 }
        return 1 / a < 0
    }
    if (wantMax) { return a > b }
    return a < b
}

// jvmMinMax picks one operand, but the DOUBLE overload of Math.max/min is
// selected as soon as one side is a double, so the answer is a double then
// (Math.max(1.5, 2) is 2.0).
function jvmMinMax(l, r, takeL) {
    var v = takeL ? l : r
    if (floIs(l) || floIs(r)) {
        var sty = jvFloStyle2(l, r)
        if (floIs(v)) { if (floStyle(v) == sty) { return v } }
        if (sty == 3) { return flo(jvF32Round(rtjNum(v)), 3) }
        return flo(rtjNum(v), sty)
    }
    return v
}

// ===================================================== the float WIDTH ======
//
// A Java `float` is a binary32, not a print style: 1.0f/3.0f is 0.33333334 where
// the double is 0.3333333333333333, and every arithmetic result has to be
// rounded back to 24 significant bits. The VALUE still travels as a double
// (every float is exactly one), so the only thing that has to go on the box is
// the width - and the tag 14 box already has a byte for a per-language print
// tag. STYLE 3 IS THE JAVA float. abnf/jsrtjvm.go's header is the specification;
// languages/lib/runtime.c is deliberately unchanged, which is why every rendering
// and every arithmetic result is intercepted HERE before the floor sees it:
// runtime.c's jf_text has no arm for style 3 and would print a float with the
// DOUBLE renderer.
//
// Three engines implement this one specification: the {__flo,__f32} box of
// languages/java-interpreter.abnf, jsJFlo{sty: floJavaF} in abnf/jsrtjvm.go, and
// this file. Nothing else builds a style 3 box, so kotlin, C# and Go are
// untouched by every line below.

// x * 2^k, EXACTLY - every factor is a power of two, so not one bit is lost.
// The chunking by 2^30 keeps the loop short for the ~280 halvings a subnormal
// float needs; a plain doubling loop would be just as exact and much slower.
function jvScale2(x, k) {
    var v = x
    var n = k
    while (n >= 30) { v = v * 1073741824; n = n - 30 }
    while (n <= 0 - 30) { v = v / 1073741824; n = n + 30 }
    while (n > 0) { v = v * 2; n = n - 1 }
    while (n < 0) { v = v / 2; n = n + 1 }
    return v
}

// jvF32Round is Math.fround: the double nearest x that a binary32 can hold,
// round-half-to-EVEN, with the subnormals and both overflows. It is what the Go
// twin spells `float64(float32(x))`.
//
// It is written on `*` and `/` by powers of two and a floor, because MetaJS has
// no access to a double's BITS - see chapter 7.7 of docs/working-on-this-project.md,
// which is the same argument that keeps d_pow and d_frexp in the C floor. The
// significand is scaled until its unit in the last place is 1, rounded there,
// and scaled back; both scalings are exact, so the single rounding is the only
// one that happens.
function jvF32Round(x) {
    if (x !== x) { return x }
    if (x === 0) { return x }
    var zero = 0
    var inf = 1 / zero
    var neg = x < 0
    var a = neg ? 0 - x : x
    if (a === inf) { return x }
    var maxf = jvScale2(16777215, 104)
    // 2^128 is above every rounding boundary and 2^-150 is the half-way point
    // below the smallest subnormal (which ties to even, i.e. to zero); both
    // early exits also bound the exponent loop below to ~280 turns.
    if (a >= jvScale2(1, 128)) { return neg ? 0 - inf : inf }
    if (a <= jvScale2(1, 0 - 150)) { return neg ? jvNegZero() : 0 }
    var e = 0
    var t = a
    while (t >= 2) { t = t / 2; e = e + 1 }
    while (t < 1) { t = t * 2; e = e - 1 }
    var q = e - 23
    if (q < 0 - 149) { q = 0 - 149 }
    var s = jvScale2(a, 0 - q)
    var f = Math.floor(s)
    var d = s - f
    if (d > 0.5) { f = f + 1 }
    if (d === 0.5) { if (f - Math.floor(f / 2) * 2 !== 0) { f = f + 1 } }
    var r = jvScale2(f, q)
    if (r > maxf) { return neg ? 0 - inf : inf }
    if (r === 0) { return neg ? jvNegZero() : 0 }
    return neg ? 0 - r : r
}

// -0.0. `0 - 0.0` is +0.0 and `x * 0` keeps an integral zero, so the working
// spelling is a division - docs/working-on-this-project.md chapter 7.10.
function jvNegZero() { return 0 / (0 - 1) }

// The result style of a binary operation on two float-ish operands. JLS 5.6.2
// promotes to the WIDER type, so `float op double` is a double; the floor's
// jf_style_of takes the LEFT operand's style, which gets that pair backwards.
function jvFloStyle2(l, r) {
    if (floIs(l)) {
        if (floStyle(l) == 3) {
            if (floIs(r)) { return floStyle(r) }
            return 3
        }
        return floStyle(l)
    }
    if (floIs(r)) { return floStyle(r) }
    return 0
}

// The fix-up applied to the floor's own floOp answer. It runs ONLY when that
// answer already claims style 3 - the one host call the double path pays - and
// style 3 can only come from a Java float, because jf_style_of took it off an
// operand. Two things are wrong with the floor's answer then, and both are
// JLS 5.6.2:
//
//   float op DOUBLE is a double. jf_style_of takes the LEFT operand's style and
//   gets that pair backwards; the VALUE is already right, since a float widens
//   to a double exactly, so only the box is rebuilt.
//
//   float op INT converts the int to a FLOAT FIRST. Rounding only the result is
//   a different answer: `float f = 0.1f; f + 16777217` is 1.6777216E7 in java
//   and 1.6777218E7 if the int keeps its exact value into the addition -
//   measured against java 24.0.2, 156 rows of a 1,231-value probe. So the
//   operation is redone on two converted operands.
function jvFloFix(op, rr, l, r) {
    if (floIs(r)) { if (floStyle(r) != 3) { return flo(floNum(rr), floStyle(r)) } }
    var a = flo(jvF32Round(rtjNum(l)), 3)
    var b = flo(jvF32Round(rtjNum(r)), 3)
    return flo(jvF32Round(floNum(rtjArith(op, a, b))), 3)
}

// JLS 5.6.2 for the RELATIONS as well: a float operand converts the other side
// to a float, so `16777217 == 16777216f` is TRUE. It answers false unless the
// promoted type really is float, so every double and every integral pair keeps
// the path it had.
function jvFloPromotes(l, r) {
    if (!floIs(l) && !floIs(r)) { return false }
    if (jvFloStyle2(l, r) != 3) { return false }
    if (!floIs(l) && !rtjIsIntegral(l)) { return false }
    if (!floIs(r) && !rtjIsIntegral(r)) { return false }
    return true
}

// ----- Float.toString -------------------------------------------------------
//
// The rule is Double.toString's, read at 24 significant bits: the SHORTEST
// decimal that reads back as the same float, plain for 1e-3 <= |v| < 1e7 and
// d.dddEnn outside it, always with a decimal point, never fewer than two
// significant digits in the scientific form.
//
// THE DIGITS COME FROM `"" + a`, NOT FROM floPrec, and that is not a style
// choice. floPrec's two implementations DISAGREE: abnf/commonscript.go answers
// exactly n digits and languages/lib/runtime.c's answers max(n, shortest), so
// floPrec(1/3, 1) is "3e-1" under llvm.Run and "3333333333333333e-1" natively.
// Using it here would have been a halves divergence in every float that needs
// fewer digits than its double. `"" + a` is the shortest round-tripping DOUBLE
// in every engine (it is what jvm_flo_str and jvmFloStr already agree on), and
// rounding that digit string down to n is exact for every n a float can want.

// The shortest round-tripping decimal of the double a > 0, as DIGITS "e" EXP
// with EXP the decimal exponent of the FIRST digit - the shape floPrec answers
// in, so the two are interchangeable at the call sites.
function jvDecDigits(a) {
    var s = "" + a
    var mant = s
    var exp = 0
    var ei = 0 - 1
    var i = 0
    while (i < s.length) {
        var c = s.charAt(i)
        if (c == "e") { ei = i }
        if (c == "E") { ei = i }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if (ei >= 0) {
        mant = s.substring(0, ei)
        var et = s.substring(ei + 1)
        if (et.charAt(0) == "+") { et = et.substring(1) }
        exp = parseInt(et, 10)
    }
    var digits = ""
    var pt = 0 - 1
    var j = 0
    while (j < mant.length) {
        var ch = mant.charAt(j)
        if (ch == ".") { pt = digits.length } else { if (ch != "-") { digits = digits + ch } }
        j = j + 1
    }
    if (pt < 0) { pt = digits.length }
    var lead = 0
    while (lead < digits.length - 1 && digits.charAt(lead) == "0") { lead = lead + 1 }
    digits = digits.substring(lead)
    pt = pt - lead
    var endz = digits.length
    while (endz > 1 && digits.charAt(endz - 1) == "0") { endz = endz - 1 }
    digits = digits.substring(0, endz)
    return digits + "e" + (pt + exp - 1)
}

// A DIGITS "e" EXP form rounded to n significant digits, half to even. The
// source is already the shortest form of the value, so a genuine tie can only
// be a decimal the double holds exactly.
function jvRoundDig(s, n) {
    var ei = s.indexOf("e")
    var ds = s.substring(0, ei)
    var ex = parseInt(s.substring(ei + 1), 10)
    if (ds.length <= n) { return s }
    var keep = ds.substring(0, n)
    var nxt = ds.charCodeAt(n) - 48
    var up = false
    if (nxt > 5) { up = true }
    if (nxt == 5) {
        var rest = false
        var i = n + 1
        while (i < ds.length) { if (ds.charCodeAt(i) != 48) { rest = true } i = i + 1 }
        if (rest) { up = true } else {
            var last = ds.charCodeAt(n - 1) - 48
            if (last - Math.floor(last / 2) * 2 == 1) { up = true }
        }
    }
    if (up) {
        var out = ""
        var carry = 1
        var k = n - 1
        while (k >= 0) {
            var d = ds.charCodeAt(k) - 48 + carry
            carry = 0
            if (d == 10) { d = 0; carry = 1 }
            out = "" + d + out
            k = k - 1
        }
        if (carry == 1) { out = ("1" + out).substring(0, n); ex = ex + 1 }
        keep = out
    }
    var endz = keep.length
    while (endz > 1 && keep.charAt(endz - 1) == "0") { endz = endz - 1 }
    return keep.substring(0, endz) + "e" + ex
}

// The value of a DIGITS "e" EXP form. At most nine digits, so the integer part
// is exact; the power of ten is applied in chunks of 10^15, the largest one a
// double holds exactly. The relative error stays under ~1e-15, which is five
// million times finer than the half-ulp of a float - and the only consumer is
// the round-trip TEST below, so an error that small cannot pick a wrong length.
function jvDecVal(s) {
    var ei = s.indexOf("e")
    var ds = s.substring(0, ei)
    var k = parseInt(s.substring(ei + 1), 10) - ds.length + 1
    var m = 0
    var i = 0
    while (i < ds.length) { m = m * 10 + (ds.charCodeAt(i) - 48); i = i + 1 }
    while (k >= 15) { m = m * 1000000000000000; k = k - 15 }
    while (k <= 0 - 15) { m = m / 1000000000000000; k = k + 15 }
    while (k > 0) { m = m * 10; k = k - 1 }
    while (k < 0) { m = m / 10; k = k + 1 }
    return m
}

// The shortest decimal that round-trips as a binary32. Nine digits always do.
function jvF32Sig(a) {
    var full = jvDecDigits(a)
    var n = 1
    while (n < 9) {
        var c = jvRoundDig(full, n)
        if (jvF32Round(jvDecVal(c)) === a) { return c }
        n = n + 1
    }
    return jvRoundDig(full, 9)
}

function jvF32Str(d) {
    if (d !== d) { return "NaN" }
    var zero = 0
    var inf = 1 / zero
    if (d === inf) { return "Infinity" }
    if (d === 0 - inf) { return "-Infinity" }
    if (d === 0) { if (1 / d < 0) { return "-0.0" } return "0.0" }
    var neg = d < 0
    var a = neg ? 0 - d : d
    var sig = jvF32Sig(a)
    var ei = sig.indexOf("e")
    var ds = sig.substring(0, ei)
    var e10 = parseInt(sig.substring(ei + 1), 10)
    var body = ""
    if (a >= 0.001 && a < 10000000) {
        body = jvF32Plain(ds, e10)
    } else {
        // Never fewer than TWO significant digits, and where the shortest form
        // has one the second is the closest two digit decimal to the ACTUAL
        // value rather than a zero: Float.MIN_VALUE is 1.401298464324817E-45,
        // which java writes "1.4E-45" and not "1.0E-45". The same rule, at the
        // same site, as Double.toString's "4.9E-324".
        if (ds.length == 1) {
            var s2 = jvRoundDig(jvDecDigits(a), 2)
            var e2 = s2.indexOf("e")
            ds = s2.substring(0, e2)
            e10 = parseInt(s2.substring(e2 + 1), 10)
        }
        var frac = ds.substring(1)
        if (frac == "") { frac = "0" }
        body = ds.charAt(0) + "." + frac + "E" + e10
    }
    if (neg) { return "-" + body }
    return body
}

// The plain form, from the digits and the exponent of the first one. The forced
// ".0" is here (100.0f is "100.0"); the two-digit minimum is NOT, because it is
// about the significand and only the scientific form shows one.
function jvF32Plain(ds, e10) {
    if (e10 >= 0) {
        var ip = ""
        var i = 0
        while (i <= e10) {
            if (i < ds.length) { ip = ip + ds.charAt(i) } else { ip = ip + "0" }
            i = i + 1
        }
        var fp = "0"
        if (ds.length > e10 + 1) { fp = ds.substring(e10 + 1) }
        return ip + "." + fp
    }
    var lead = "0."
    var k = 0 - e10 - 1
    while (k > 0) { lead = lead + "0"; k = k - 1 }
    return lead + ds
}

// Every rendering of a tag 14 box goes through here, because the floor cannot
// render style 3 (see the section header).
function jvFloText(v) {
    if (floStyle(v) == 3) { return jvF32Str(floNum(v)) }
    return floStr(v)
}

// js_jflo32 is the only new extern the width needs: `(float) e`, a `1.0f`
// literal and a `float x = e` declaration all lower to it. Its Go twin is
// registered in abnf/jsrtjvm.go.
function js_jflo32(v) { return flo(jvF32Round(rtjNum(v)), 3) }

// ===================================================== rendering ============

// jvpStr is String.valueOf: what println writes and what `+` concatenates.
function jpStr(v) { return jpRender(v, 0) }

function jpRender(v, depth) {
    if (depth > 32) { return "..." }
    if (v === undefined) { return "null" }
    if (v === null) { return "null" }
    if (typeof v == "string") { return v }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return v ? "true" : "false" }
    if (floIs(v)) { return jvFloText(v) }
    // sintStr is si_str, which prints a SIGNED cell with its minus sign - the
    // long arm this used to need (jlStr) is the floor's own now.
    if (sintIs(v)) { return sintStr(v) }
    if (typeof v == "number") { return "" + v }
    if (typeof v == "function") { return "" + v }
    if (Array.isArray(v)) { return jpArrayTag(v) + "@" + jpHash(v) }
    if (jvIsChar(v)) { return String.fromCharCode(v["__char"]) }
    return jpObj(v, depth)
}

// jpObj renders the object shapes the two Java grammars build.
function jpObj(o, depth) {
    // A user toString() wins over every built-in rendering, as it does in Java.
    // The __class/__super walk is SHARED - rtFindMethod, lib/runtime.metajs. This
    // file called it 400 lines below and STILL carried a second private copy of
    // the same eleven lines, as csharp-rt.metajs did under the name
    // cpFindToString. Byte-identical after normalising the identifiers. Zero
    // knobs.
    var f = rtFindMethod(o["__class"], "toString")
    if (f !== undefined) { return jpRender(f.apply(undefined, [o]), depth + 1) }
    // The class descriptor itself.
    if (o["__isclass"] === true) {
        var cn = o["__name"]
        if (typeof cn == "string") { return "class " + cn }
    }
    var cls = o["__class"]
    if (cls === undefined) { return jpPlainObj(o) }
    if (cls === null) { return jpPlainObj(o) }
    if (typeof cls != "object") { return jpPlainObj(o) }
    // An enum constant without an override prints its constant name (the
    // java.lang.Enum#toString contract).
    var en = o["__ename"]
    if (typeof en == "string") { return en }
    // A record prints its canonical form, P[x=1, y=2] (java.lang.Record). The
    // name is the SIMPLE one: the generated toString uses getSimpleName, so a
    // nested record is `P[...]` and not `Main$P[...]`.
    var comps = cls["__record"]
    if (comps !== undefined) {
        if (Array.isArray(comps)) {
            var out = ""
            var i = 0
            while (i < comps.length) {
                if (i > 0) { out = out + ", " }
                out = out + comps[i] + "=" + jpRender(o[comps[i]], depth + 1)
                i = i + 1
            }
            var simple = cls["__name"]
            if (typeof simple != "string") { simple = "Object" }
            return simple + "[" + out + "]"
        }
    }
    return jpClassName(cls) + "@" + jpHash(o)
}

// The Go twin's fallthrough for an object with no __class is rt.toString, which
// is JavaScript's "[object Object]".
function jpPlainObj(o) { return "[object Object]" }

// jpClassName is the class' BINARY name: a nested type is spelled Outer$Inner,
// the way java.lang.Class#getName does, and the grammars record that as __bname.
function jpClassName(cls) {
    var b = cls["__bname"]
    if (typeof b == "string") { if (b.length > 0) { return b } }
    var n = cls["__name"]
    if (typeof n == "string") { return n }
    return "Object"
}

// jpArrayTag is the array's class name as getName spells it. The element type is
// INFERRED from the elements: this value model does not carry the declared one,
// and the Go twin makes the same inference from the same table.
function jpArrayTag(a) {
    var tag = ""
    var i = 0
    while (i < a.length) {
        var t = jpElemTag(a[i])
        if (t == "") { return "[Ljava.lang.Object;" }
        if (tag != "") { if (t != tag) { return "[Ljava.lang.Object;" } }
        tag = t
        i = i + 1
    }
    if (tag == "") { return "[Ljava.lang.Object;" }
    return tag
}

function jpElemTag(v) {
    if (jvIsChar(v)) { return "[C" }
    if (typeof v == "boolean") { return "[Z" }
    if (typeof v == "string") { return "[Ljava.lang.String;" }
    if (floIs(v)) { if (floStyle(v) == 3) { return "[F" } return "[D" }
    if (sintIs(v)) {
        var w = sintWidth(v)
        if (w == 64) { return "[J" }
        if (w == 16) { return "[S" }
        return "[B"
    }
    if (typeof v == "number") { return "[I" }
    return ""
}

// ----- identity hash --------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every object rendered with an `@` gets a stable per-run number. Real java
// prints System.identityHashCode, which is address derived and changes between
// two runs of the SAME program, so no assertion can depend on the digits; what
// the halves must agree on is that one object renders the same way twice and
// that a run is deterministic, which a counter gives. The Go twin keys a map by
// the value; MetaJS has no such map, so the objects are kept in a parallel array
// and found by IDENTITY - which is the same test a Go map on a pointer does.

var jpIdObjs = []
var jpIdNums = []

function jpHash(v) {
    var i = 0
    while (i < jpIdObjs.length) {
        if (jpIdObjs[i] === v) { return jpHex(439041024 + jpIdNums[i]) }
        i = i + 1
    }
    var n = jpIdObjs.length + 1
    jpIdObjs.push(v)
    jpIdNums.push(n)
    return jpHex(439041024 + n)
}

// %x of a non-negative integer. 439041024 is 0x1a2b3c00, the Go twin's base.
function jpHex(n) {
    var digits = "0123456789abcdef"
    var v = n
    var out = ""
    if (v == 0) { return "0" }
    while (v > 0) {
        var d = v - Math.floor(v / 16) * 16
        out = digits.charAt(d) + out
        v = Math.floor(v / 16)
    }
    return out
}

// ===================================================== java.lang boxes ======

// jvBoxType builds one of java.lang's boxed-primitive types. MIN_VALUE /
// MAX_VALUE are the whole reason a sized integer had to exist: Long.MAX_VALUE
// cannot be written as a plain number at all.
function jvBoxType(name) {
    var o = {}
    if (name == "Integer") {
        o["MAX_VALUE"] = 2147483647
        o["MIN_VALUE"] = 0 - 2147483648
        jvAddCommon(o, 32, 32, 4)
        o["parseInt"] = function(s) { return jvParseInt(jpStr(s), 32) }
        return o
    }
    if (name == "Long") {
        o["MAX_VALUE"] = jlMaxL()
        o["MIN_VALUE"] = jlMinL()
        jvAddCommon(o, 64, 64, 8)
        o["parseLong"] = function(s) { return jvParseInt(jpStr(s), 64) }
        return o
    }
    if (name == "Byte") {
        o["MAX_VALUE"] = sint(0, 127, 8, 0)
        o["MIN_VALUE"] = sint(0, 128, 8, 0)
        jvAddCommon(o, 8, 8, 1)
        return o
    }
    if (name == "Short") {
        o["MAX_VALUE"] = sint(0, 32767, 16, 0)
        o["MIN_VALUE"] = sint(0, 32768, 16, 0)
        jvAddCommon(o, 16, 16, 2)
        return o
    }
    // jvmMathObject (abnf/jsrtjvm.go): the `Math` a compiled Java program sees.
    // The handle runtime's own Math is JavaScript's and answers a plain number for
    // everything, which would make Math.abs(-2.0) print "2" where Java prints
    // "2.0". Only the three methods the Java subset documents are provided, which
    // is what java-interpreter.abnf's hostGlobals["Math"] has too.
    //
    // In the Go half this object is the HOST GLOBAL __jmath and is reached by a
    // scope lookup; the C floor seeds no such name, so java-to-llvm-ir.abnf probes
    // the scope and falls back to this descriptor (see jvScopeProbe there).
    if (name == "Math") {
        // Math.abs is OVERLOADED on the argument type and answers THAT type:
        // abs(long) is a long, abs(int) an int, abs(double) a double. A long
        // must therefore not take the int overload's 32 bit wrap - it made
        // Math.abs(3000000000L) answer -1294967296 where java 24 says
        // 3000000000, and Math.abs(Long.MAX_VALUE) answer 0 - and it cannot go
        // through rtjNum either, which is a double and loses the low bits past
        // 2^53. Negating in 64 bit two's complement is exact and maps
        // Long.MIN_VALUE to ITSELF, which is Java's answer and not an error
        // (JLS 15.15.4: there is no positive long of that magnitude).
        //
        // byte / short / char are NOT excluded here on purpose: Java widens all
        // three to int before the call, and rtWrap32 of the plain magnitude is
        // exactly that int.
        o["abs"] = function(v) {
            if (floIs(v)) { return floAbs(v) }
            if (jvIsLong(v)) {
                if (sintCmp(v, jlZero()) < 0) { return jlNeg(v) }
                return v
            }
            return rtWrap32(Math.abs(rtjNum(v)))
        }
        o["max"] = function(a, b) { return jvmMinMax(a, b, jvmTakeL(rtjNum(a), rtjNum(b), true)) }
        o["min"] = function(a, b) { return jvmMinMax(a, b, jvmTakeL(rtjNum(a), rtjNum(b), false)) }
        return o
    }
    if (name == "Character") {
        o["MAX_VALUE"] = jvChar(65535)
        o["MIN_VALUE"] = jvChar(0)
        o["SIZE"] = 16
        o["BYTES"] = 2
        return o
    }
    return o
}

function jvAddCommon(o, width, size, bytes) {
    o["SIZE"] = size
    o["BYTES"] = bytes
    o["valueOf"] = function(v) { return jvNarrow(v, width) }
    o["toString"] = function(v) { return jpStr(v) }
    o["compare"] = function(a, b) {
        if (jvCmp("<", a, b)) { return 0 - 1 }
        if (jvCmp(">", a, b)) { return 1 }
        return 0
    }
    o["max"] = function(a, b) {
        if (jvCmp(">", a, b)) { return jvNarrow(a, width) }
        return jvNarrow(b, width)
    }
    o["min"] = function(a, b) {
        if (jvCmp("<", a, b)) { return jvNarrow(a, width) }
        return jvNarrow(b, width)
    }
}

// Integer.parseInt / Long.parseLong: a decimal digit run with an optional sign,
// and a NumberFormatException - here a plain abort, since the message is the only
// part a program reads - on anything else.
function jvParseInt(s, width) {
    var t = s.trim()
    var neg = false
    var i = 0
    if (t.length > 0) {
        var c0 = t.charAt(0)
        if (c0 == "+") { i = 1 } else if (c0 == "-") {
            neg = true
            i = 1
        }
    }
    if (i >= t.length) {
        fail("java.lang.NumberFormatException: For input string: \"" + s + "\"")
        return 0
    }
    var acc = jlZero()
    var ten = jlFromNum(10)
    while (i < t.length) {
        var d = t.charCodeAt(i) - 48
        if (d < 0) {
            fail("java.lang.NumberFormatException: For input string: \"" + s + "\"")
            return 0
        }
        if (d > 9) {
            fail("java.lang.NumberFormatException: For input string: \"" + s + "\"")
            return 0
        }
        acc = jlOp(0, jlOp(2, acc, ten), jlFromNum(d))
        i = i + 1
    }
    if (neg) { acc = jlNeg(acc) }
    return jvNarrow(acc, width)
}

// ===================================================== the exports ==========
//
// Everything below is a TOP-LEVEL `function js_*`, which is what -rt-lib exports
// under its own C symbol. The order follows abnf/jsrtjava.go and the shared block
// of abnf/jsrt.go.

// ----- the language-NEUTRAL nine --------------------------------------------
// None of these is Java's. They live in abnf/jsrt.go, several languages emit
// them, and they are here only because the C floor does not have them - see the
// extern split in docs/runtime-next-plan.md part 3.

// js_arr_new_n is SHARED with csharp - lib/runtime-jvm.metajs. Once jvNum and
// csNum turned out to be the same function, the two copies had zero differences.

// js_char is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs; it was the same one-line delegation to
// the char boxer in csharp, java and kotlin, and the _raw suffix travels with it.

// The shared dynamic type test behind instanceof. abnf/jsrt.go's isTypeName is
// the spec, including the part that surprises: the Int/Long/Short/Byte arm tests
// for a PLAIN number, so a boxed long is NOT `instanceof Long` there either.
// js_is_type is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs, via the rtkIsChar/rtkIsArr hooks.

// Java/C# +: string concat, 32 bit add for two int values, float add otherwise.
// The STRING rendering here is abnf/jsrt.go's javaString, not jsrtjava.go's
// jvpStr - js_jvadd below is the one that renders through the Java printer.
// js_jadd is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs, via the rtkStr/rtkNum hooks above.


// Java & | ^: the non-short-circuit boolean operator when both sides are
// booleans, the int32 bit operation otherwise.
//
// DUPLICATED, DELIBERATELY, with kotlin-rt.metajs's js_jband, and the difference
// list is ONE item that NOTHING CAN REACH. The bodies differ only in the int32
// coercion (jvI here, k1I32 there - two integer models, one per value model),
// and NEITHER EMITTER EVER PRODUCES A CALL THAT REACHES IT: `&` lowers to
// js_jvarith / js_ktarith (java-to-llvm-ir.abnf:2606, kotlin-to-llvm-ir.abnf:4098)
// and the ONLY js_jband either grammar emits is the boolean fold of a structural
// equality (java:3934, kotlin:6682), which returns from the arm above.
//
// So the four lines are shared in every sense but the text, and merging them
// still costs more than they are worth: kotlin does not import
// lib/runtime-jvm.metajs (it has neither rtjkCmp nor rtjkFloOperand and wants
// none of that file's six bodies), and a home in lib/runtime.metajs would need an
// ELEVENTH rtk* hook declared in all ten importers to carry a coercion that is
// dead in both callers. Re-judged in the fifth pass, after the declaration tax
// was measured at zero, and declined on the plumbing rather than on the tax.
//
// NOTE FOR WHEN THE ARM WAKES UP: the Go twin (abnf/jsrt.go:5781) has ONE
// js_jband for both languages and coerces with rt.toInt32 for both. If an
// emitter ever routes a non-boolean here, THAT is where java and kotlin will
// disagree with their own twin - and they will disagree in two directions.
function js_jband(l, r) {
    if (typeof l == "boolean") { if (typeof r == "boolean") { return l && r } }
    return jvI(l) & jvI(r)
}

// Java's String.charAt: a CHAR, not a one-character String - so s.charAt(1) + 0
// is 101 and not "e0" (JLS 4.2.1, char is an integral type).
function js_jcharat(target, args) {
    if (typeof target == "string") {
        var i = rtjNum(args[0])
        if (i < 0 || i >= target.length) {
            fail("string index " + i + " out of range for \"" + target + "\"")
            return undefined
        }
        return jvChar(target.charCodeAt(i))
    }
    return js_mcall(target, "charAt", args)
}

// Java's == when a char may be involved. String.charAt keeps answering a
// one-character String in the interpreter's world, so a char must compare equal
// to that too. Without a char operand this is exactly js_seq.
function js_jchareq(l, r) {
    if (jvIsChar(l)) {
        if (typeof r == "string") { return r.length == 1 && r.charCodeAt(0) == l["__char"] }
        if (jvIsChar(r)) { return l["__char"] === r["__char"] }
        if (rtIsPlainNum(r)) { return l["__char"] === r }
        if (sintIs(r)) { return jvCmp("==", l, r) }
        if (floIs(r)) { return floEq(flo(l["__char"], 0), r) }
        return false
    }
    if (jvIsChar(r)) { return js_jchareq(r, l) }
    return rtjStrictEq(l, r)
}

// The floor's === with the two boxes taught to it - a sized integer and a boxed
// double are both `typeof "number"`, and MetaJS's own === on two boxes is
// identity, so the numeric cases are spelled out - is rtjStrictEq, SHARED with
// csharp in lib/runtime-jvm.metajs, its ONE knob being rtjkCmp. Its sized-integer
// arm is a FIX the merge found: floEq (jf_num_eq / jvmNumEq) has no tag 13 case,
// so `long == double` answered false in BOTH compiler engines while both
// interpreter halves answered true. See the note at the shared body.

// (target, method name, args array). The Go twin (rt.memberCall in abnf/jsrt.go)
// is a LANGUAGE-NEUTRAL dispatcher; only the arms java-to-llvm-ir.abnf can reach
// are here, and the ones it cannot are named at the fallthrough rather than
// guessed at.
function js_mcall(recv, name, args) {
    if (typeof recv == "string") {
        if (name == "length") { return recv.length }
        if (name == "charAt") {
            var ci = rtjNum(args[0])
            if (ci < 0 || ci >= recv.length) {
                fail("charAt(" + ci + ") out of range for \"" + recv + "\"")
                return undefined
            }
            return recv.charAt(ci)
        }
        if (name == "equals") { return rtjStrictEq(recv, args[0]) }
        if (name == "substring") {
            var b = args.length > 0 ? rtjNum(args[0]) : 0
            var e = args.length > 1 ? rtjNum(args[1]) : recv.length
            return rtSubstring(recv, b, e)
        }
        if (name == "indexOf") { return recv.indexOf(jpStr(args[0])) }
        if (name == "isEmpty") { return recv.length == 0 }
        fail("unknown String method: " + name)
        return undefined
    }
    if (recv === undefined || recv === null) {
        fail("method call '" + name + "' on a " + (recv === null ? "null" : "undefined"))
        return undefined
    }
    if (typeof recv != "object") {
        fail("method call '" + name + "' on a " + typeof recv)
        return undefined
    }
    if (Array.isArray(recv)) {
        if (name == "length") { return recv.length }
        if (name == "size") { return recv.length }
        if (name == "add") {
            recv.push(args[0])
            return true
        }
        if (name == "get") {
            var gi = rtjNum(args[0])
            if (gi < 0 || gi >= recv.length) {
                fail("list index " + gi + " out of range")
                return undefined
            }
            return recv[gi]
        }
        if (name == "contains") {
            var k = 0
            while (k < recv.length) {
                if (rtjStrictEq(recv[k], args[0])) { return true }
                k = k + 1
            }
            return false
        }
        fail("unknown list method '" + name + "'")
        return undefined
    }
    if (jvIsChar(recv)) {
        fail("method call '" + name + "' on a char")
        return undefined
    }
    var cls = recv["__class"]
    if (cls !== undefined) {
        // The __class/__super walk is SHARED - rtFindMethod, lib/runtime.metajs.
        var mm = rtFindMethod(cls, name)
        if (typeof mm == "function") { return mm.apply(undefined, rtPrepend(recv, args)) }
        fail("unknown method '" + name + "' on an instance")
        return undefined
    }
    var f = recv[name]
    if (f !== undefined && typeof f == "function") { return f.apply(undefined, args) }
    fail("unknown method '" + name + "'")
    return undefined
}

// jpPrepend is SHARED as rtPrepend - lib/runtime.metajs.

// String.substring with the Go twin's substringRange clamping.
// js_supercall is SHARED - lib/runtime.metajs (four languages, one knob: rtkCall).

// ----- Java's own sixteen ---------------------------------------------------

// The ArithmeticException class descriptor, handed over once at module init so a
// divide by zero can THROW rather than abort.
function js_jvexc(cls) {
    if (cls !== undefined && cls !== null && typeof cls == "object") { jvArithExcCls = cls }
    return undefined
}

// One binary integral operator. A double operand keeps the FLOAT path, which is
// where the two boxes meet.
function js_jvarith(op, l, r) {
    // The one host call the DOUBLE path pays for the float width: floStyle of a
    // result that is not style 3 is the whole test, because style 3 can only
    // have come off a Java float operand.
    if (floIs(l) || floIs(r)) { var rf = rtjArith(op, l, r); if (floStyle(rf) == 3) { return jvFloFix(op, rf, l, r) } return rf }
    if (!rtjIsIntegral(l) || !rtjIsIntegral(r)) {
        // & | ^ on two booleans are Java's non-short-circuit boolean operators;
        // anything else is not a program javac accepts.
        if (typeof l == "boolean" && typeof r == "boolean") {
            if (op == "&") { return l && r }
            if (op == "|") { return l || r }
            if (op == "^") { return l !== r }
        }
        var rg = rtjArith(op, l, r)
        if (floStyle(rg) == 3) { return jvFloFix(op, rg, l, r) }
        return rg
    }
    return jvArith(op, l, r)
}

function js_jvshift(op, l, r) { return jvShift(op, l, r) }

// An ordered comparison. Two integers compare at the promoted width; anything
// else (a double, a string pair) keeps the shared comparison, rtCompare.
//
// A DEFECT WAS HERE, in BOTH ENGINES, and the two halves gave two DIFFERENT
// wrong answers. rtCompare is abnf/jsrt.go:1530's jsCompare, which answers the
// SENTINEL 2 when either operand coerces to NaN and says at the site that it
// means "every relation is false". The old jpCompare - deleted, it was the
// fourth copy of this body - dropped the sentinel and fell through to `return
// 0`, i.e. EQUAL, and its comment claimed that was "the Go twin's behaviour
// exactly". It was not. Read by the four tests below:
//
//     sentinel 2 (the Go twin)     <  false   >  TRUE    <= false   >= TRUE
//     0          (old layer 2)     <  false   >  false   <= TRUE    >= TRUE
//     JLS 15.20.1 (real java)      <  false   >  false   <= false   >= false
//
// Measured against java 24 on a fourteen-row probe (NaN and 1.0 in both orders,
// NaN against itself, and the float NaN): java 24 says false everywhere except
// `!=`; the GO TWIN was wrong on 4 rows, LAYER 2 on 6, and the two halves
// disagreed with EACH OTHER on 6 - which --cross would have caught had it ever
// probed a NaN relation, and --full has no such assertion. This is the same
// defect docs/runtime-merge-plan.md fixed for C# in Part A; java was the twin of
// it and was not looked at. abnf/jsrtjava.go:467 takes the same three lines.
// Smoothing the 2 to 0 in rtCompare is still wrong and still must not be done:
// 0 makes `<=` and `>=` true.
// The body is rtjRel - SHARED with csharp's js_cscmp, lib/runtime-jvm.metajs, so
// that the fix below cannot be made in one language and not the other a third
// time.
// A float operand promotes the other side to float too (JLS 5.6.2), so
// `16777217 < 16777216f` is false - the int converts to 1.6777216E7 first.
function js_jvcmp(op, l, r) {
    // THE INTEGRAL FAST PATH IS SPELLED OUT HERE rather than reached through
    // rtjRel, and that is a MEASUREMENT, not a preference. `i < 40000` is the
    // whole of tests/bench/mod.java's loop condition, and asking the float
    // question first cost java +5.32% against a 4.96% spread over 9 draws. The
    // arm below is rtjRel's own first line with rtjkCmp expanded (it is jvCmp
    // here), so it is the same answer by construction - and the integer path now
    // does two rtjIsIntegral calls where it used to do two more floIs on top.
    if (rtjIsIntegral(l) && rtjIsIntegral(r)) { return jvCmp(op, l, r) }
    if (jvFloPromotes(l, r)) { return rtjRel(op, flo(jvF32Round(rtjNum(l)), 3), flo(jvF32Round(rtjNum(r)), 3)) }
    return rtjRel(op, l, r)
}

// js_jchareq with the same promotion. The emitter calls THIS for `==` and `!=`;
// js_jchareq itself stays as it is, because it is shared with kotlin (which has
// no float width) and the switch-label site still uses it. (The record-component
// site used to; it calls js_jvaleq below now.)
function js_jvchareq(l, r) {
    // Two host calls before the delegation, for the same reason js_jvcmp spells
    // its integral arm out: everything that is not a float box must reach
    // js_jchareq without paying for the promotion test.
    if (floIs(l) || floIs(r)) {
        if (jvFloPromotes(l, r)) { return jvF32Round(rtjNum(l)) == jvF32Round(rtjNum(r)) }
    }
    return js_jchareq(l, r)
}

// ONE component of a record's canonical equals, per JLS 8.10.3 - and none of the
// three arms is ===. The twin is m["js_jvaleq"] in abnf/jsrtjava.go, arm for arm.
//
//   a double / float component   Double.compare / Float.compare == 0, so NaN
//                                EQUALS NaN and +0.0 does NOT equal -0.0
//   any other primitive          == , chars included (js_jchareq)
//   a reference                  Objects.equals(a, b): identity, then the
//                                component's OWN equals - which for a nested
//                                record is the generated one, so the recursion
//                                is a DISPATCH and this file needs no loop.
//
// A class that declares no equals, and an array (which does not override it),
// keep identity, because Object.equals is identity.
function js_jvaleq(a, b) {
    if (floIs(a) && floIs(b)) { return jvDblCmpEq(floNum(a), floNum(b)) }
    if (js_jchareq(a, b) === true) { return true }
    if (a === undefined || a === null) { return false }
    if (typeof a != "object") { return false }
    if (Array.isArray(a)) { return false }
    var mm = rtFindMethod(a["__class"], "equals")
    if (typeof mm != "function") { return false }
    return mm.apply(undefined, rtPrepend(a, [b])) === true
}

// Double.compare(x, y) == 0, i.e. doubleToLongBits equality: one NaN, and the
// sign of a zero is part of the value. ONE body serves both widths - a float box
// already holds the binary32-rounded value, and two distinct binary32 values are
// two distinct doubles. The sign of a zero is invisible to a comparison, so it is
// read the way dart-rt.metajs's dtSignBit reads it.
function jvDblCmpEq(x, y) {
    if (x !== x) { return y !== y }
    if (y !== y) { return false }
    if (x === 0 && y === 0) { return jvSignBit(x) === jvSignBit(y) }
    return x === y
}
function jvSignBit(d) {
    if (d < 0) { return true }
    if (d === 0) { return 1 / d < 0 }
    return false
}

// A cast or a declared integral type: (value, bits).
function js_jvconv(v, bits) { return jvNarrow(v, rtjNum(bits)) }

// A declared type applied to an initializer: only an INTEGRAL value narrows, so
// `Object o = "s"` and a lambda pass through untouched.
function js_jvadopt(v, bits) {
    if (!rtjIsIntegral(v)) { return v }
    return jvNarrow(v, rtjNum(bits))
}

// An integral literal, passed as DIGITS: emitNum would already have rounded
// 9223372036854775807 to 9223372036854776000 on the way into the module.
// (text, radix, isLong).
function js_jvlit(text, radix, isLong) {
    var rx = rtjNum(radix)
    var acc = jlZero()
    var rb = jlFromNum(rx)
    var i = 0
    while (i < text.length) {
        var c = text.charCodeAt(i)
        var d = 0 - 1
        if (c >= 48 && c <= 57) { d = c - 48 } else if (c >= 97 && c <= 102) { d = c - 97 + 10 } else if (c >= 65 && c <= 70) { d = c - 65 + 10 }
        if (d >= 0 && d < rx) { acc = jlOp(0, jlOp(2, acc, rb), jlFromNum(d)) }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if (isLong) { return acc }
    return jvNarrow(acc, 32)
}

// -x and ~x, both at the PROMOTED width (JLS 5.6.1): a long stays a long,
// everything integral else becomes an int.
function js_jvneg(v) {
    // NOT `flo(0 - floNum(v), 0)`. Unary minus on a double is emitted as
    // `0.0 - x` by languages/c-to-llvm-ir.abnf, and 0.0 - 0.0 is +0.0 - so the
    // sign of a NEGATIVE ZERO would be lost and "" + (-0.0) would print "0.0".
    // The same trap languages/lib/runtime.c's d_ldexp records. Multiplying by
    // -1.0 computes the sign instead of writing it, and is exact for every
    // operand including the infinities and NaN.
    if (floIs(v)) { return floOp(2, v, flo(0 - 1, 0)) }
    if (jvIsLong(v)) { return jlNeg(v) }
    return rtWrap32(0 - jvI(v))
}

function js_jvnot(v) {
    if (jvIsLong(v)) { return jlNot(v) }
    return rtWrap32(0 - jvI(v) - 1)
}

// ++ / -- keep the type of what they step, which is the same implicit narrowing
// a compound assignment carries (JLS 15.14.2).
function js_jvstep(v, d) {
    var n = rtjNum(d)
    if (jvIsChar(v)) { return jvChar((v["__char"] + n) & 65535) }
    // ++/-- keeps the operand's own type, WIDTH included: a float steps as a
    // float, so `float f = 16777216f; f++` stays 1.6777216E7.
    if (floIs(v)) { var st = floStyle(v); if (st == 3) { return flo(jvF32Round(floNum(v) + n), 3) } return flo(floNum(v) + n, st) }
    if (sintIs(v)) { return jvNarrow(jvArith("+", v, jvStepDelta(v, n)), sintWidth(v)) }
    return rtWrap32(jvI(v) + n)
}

// The step amount at the operand's own width, so a long steps by a long.
function jvStepDelta(v, n) {
    if (jvIsLong(v)) { return jlFromNum(n) }
    return n
}

// The result of a COMPOUND assignment: the operation, then the implicit
// narrowing cast back to the LEFT operand's type (JLS 15.26.2). So
// `byte b = 10; b += 300` is (byte)310 == 54 and `int i = 5; i += 1.5` is
// (int)6.5 == 6. A String left operand keeps its concatenation.
function js_jvnarrowlike(l, v) {
    if (floIs(l)) {
        // A compound assignment carries an implicit cast back to the LEFT
        // operand's type (JLS 15.26.2), so `float f = 1.1f; f += 0.1` is
        // (float)(f + 0.1) and NOT a double.
        if (floStyle(l) == 3) { return flo(jvF32Round(rtjNum(v)), 3) }
        if (floIs(v)) { return v }
        return flo(rtjNum(v), floStyle(l))
    }
    if (jvIsChar(l)) { return jvChar(jvLow32(v) & 65535) }
    if (sintIs(l)) { return jvNarrow(v, sintWidth(l)) }
    if (rtIsPlainNum(l)) {
        if (rtjIsIntegral(v) || floIs(v)) { return jvNarrow(v, 32) }
    }
    return v
}

// The boxed-primitive types Integer / Long / Byte / Short / Character.
function js_jvboxtype(name) { return jvBoxType(name) }

// A (char) cast: narrow to 16 bits and re-box. char is the one integral type
// that is UNSIGNED, so the mask rather than a sign extension is the whole answer
// - (char)-1 is 65535 and (char)70000 is 4464.
function js_jvchar(v) { return jvChar(jvLow32(jvNarrow(v, 64)) & 65535) }

// The plain-number reading a raw consumer needs (an array index, a length, a
// bound, and the operand of the FLOOR's js_jflo): a box is not a double and
// every such consumer wants one.
//
// The Go twin converts a jsGInt and passes everything else through, because its
// consumers already understand jsChar and jsJFlo. The C floor does not know the
// {__char} box at all, so that one is still converted here; the sized-integer
// arm is now REDUNDANT for correctness - a signed tag 13 cell reads correctly
// through to_number - and is kept because it is also what js_jflo's Go twin
// does, and because a raw consumer wants a plain number rather than a cell.
// Everything else still passes through unchanged, which is what keeps
// `(double) "1"` reading the same in both halves.
function js_jvnum(v) {
    if (sintIs(v)) { return rtjNum(v) }
    if (jvIsChar(v)) { return v["__char"] }
    if (floIs(v)) { return floNum(v) }
    return v
}

// System.out.println(x) / System.out.print(x). The argument ARRAY of the emitted
// closure arrives whole, so the no-argument println(), which writes just the line
// terminator, is distinguishable from println(null).
function js_jvprint(args) {
    println(jpFirst(args))
    return undefined
}

function js_jvwrite(args) {
    print(jpFirst(args))
    return undefined
}

function jpFirst(args) {
    if (!Array.isArray(args)) { return jpStr(args) }
    if (args.length == 0) { return "" }
    return jpStr(args[0])
}

// String.valueOf(x) and the string form used by `+`.
function js_jvstr(v) { return jpStr(v) }

// Java's `+`. Identical to js_jadd except that the string case renders through
// the Java printer, so `"" + arr` and `"" + obj` agree with println instead of
// falling through to JavaScript's ToString.
function js_jvadd(l, r) {
    if (typeof l == "string" || typeof r == "string") { return jpStr(l) + jpStr(r) }
    if (floIs(l) || floIs(r)) { var rp = rtjArith("+", l, r); if (floStyle(rp) == 3) { return jvFloFix("+", rp, l, r) } return rp }
    if (rtjIsIntegral(l) && rtjIsIntegral(r)) { return jvArith("+", l, r) }
    return jvGenericAdd(l, r)
}

// The shared jsAdd for the operand pairs Java's own arms do not cover: anything
// that is not a string and not integral coerces to a number.
function jvGenericAdd(l, r) { return rtjNum(l) + rtjNum(r) }
